---
/**
 * "In the API" — the operations that do, from your own code, what this page describes in words.
 *
 * Dan, 2026-08-24: "I also think we do need this section as well, because there will be some items
 * that are specific, like API endpoints that are specific to a doc. Most of them I think will be
 * generic, but there will be some where we should add that."
 *
 * ── IT NAMES OPERATIONS, NOT PATHS, AND THAT IS NOT AN OMISSION ────────────────────────────────
 * `docs/api/index.md` carries the ruling this component obeys, and it is worth quoting because the
 * obvious way to build this violates it: "Inventing a plausible set would be worse than a banner,
 * because a banner is detectable and an invented endpoint is not: a reader has no way to tell
 * `GET /v1/runs` from something we made up to fill a table, and neither has the next person to edit
 * this file."
 *
 * The API surface is not settled — there are no paths, parameter names or error codes to write down
 * yet. What IS settled is the object model the glossary publishes and what each page's subject
 * DOES, so this section says the second in words. The day a reference page exists, the author adds
 * `slug` to the frontmatter entry and the row becomes a link. Nothing here has to be corrected then;
 * something is added.
 *
 * ── WHY IT SITS IN THE FOOTER RATHER THAN IN THE PROSE ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * An author would otherwise have to import a component into a `.md` file, which none of the other
 * ninety pages do and which would not survive the first author who forgot. Frontmatter plus a
 * footer render means the section cannot be half-added: declare the field and it is there, in the
 * same place, on every page that declares it. It is ABOVE the rating block because it belongs to the
 * article, and the rating is the first thing that is about the article rather than in it.
 *
 * ── THE FIELD AND ITS READER SHIP TOGETHER, AND A GATE WATCHES ─────────────────────────────────
 * `content.config.ts` records two frontmatter flags that were declared, read by nothing, and left
 * pages silently unchanged at HTTP 200 with every gate green. `smoke-support` asserts that every
 * page whose frontmatter declares `api` renders this block, and that no page renders it without.
 */
import Icon from './Icon.astro';

interface Props {
  api?: { summary: string; slug?: string }[];
}

const { api } = Astro.props;
---

{
  api && api.length > 0 && (
    <section class="bo-docsapi" data-docs-api aria-labelledby="bo-docsapi-h">
      <h2 class="bo-docsapi-h" id="bo-docsapi-h">
        <Icon name="plug" size={16} />
        <span>In the API</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="bo-docsapi-lede">
        What this page describes, done from your own code. The{' '}
        <a href="/api/">API reference</a> is where these are written up.
      </p>
      <ul class="bo-docsapi-list">
        {api.map((op) => (
          <li>{op.slug ? <a href={`/${op.slug}/`}>{op.summary}</a> : op.summary}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  )
}

<style>
  .bo-docsapi {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 20px 24px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-12);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
  }

  .bo-docsapi-h {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
    border: 0;
  }

  .bo-docsapi-lede {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* THE ROWS ARE A LIST, NOT A TABLE. A table needs a second column, and the second column is the
     path, which is the thing this section is forbidden to guess at. When paths exist the row becomes
     a link and the shape does not have to change. */
  .bo-docsapi-list {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    padding: 0 0 0 20px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }
  .bo-docsapi-list li + li {
    margin-top: 4px;
  }
</style>
